  const GEMS = [{"id": "botifarra-dol-a", "pattern": "botifarra dolça", "tag": "Food gem · Sweet", "name": "Botifarra dolça", "story": "Europe’s charcuterie map holds one real anomaly — a pork sausage cured with sugar, lemon peel and sometimes cinnamon — and it exists only round Girona. Barcelona files it as an Empordà speciality; the food historian Jaume Fàbrega puts its heart in Girona itself, with La Selva, the Pla de l’Estany and part of the Garrotxa in on it. It reached print late, in Ferran Agulló’s 1930s description, and Josep Pla’s monastic origin has no proof. Born of the farmhouse matança, it carries the Productes de l’Empordà guarantee mark (2003) and is eaten caramelised with apple, as pudding.", "where": "The charcuterie stalls of the Mercat del Lleó; in Empordà country restaurants, at the end of the meal."}, {"id": "anxova-de-l-escala", "pattern": "Anxoves de l", "tag": "Food gem · Fish", "name": "Anxova de l'Escala", "story": "L’Escala began in the sixteenth century as a fishermen’s quarter of Empúries, whose ruins hold a first-century salting factory. Its fortune was salt: once the Alfolí, the salt store, was finished in 1697, ships landed salt on the beach, barrels of anchovy sailed out, and the population quadrupled in the eighteenth century. More than a hundred lateen-rigged sardinal boats once worked the bay. The town’s archivist documents salting without a break since the 1700s.", "where": "The salting houses of L’Escala, forty minutes north-east — the Costa Brava guide has them."}, {"id": "formatge-garrotxa", "pattern": "Garrotxa", "tag": "Food gem · Cheese", "name": "Formatge Garrotxa", "story": "Garrotxa the cheese very nearly outlived the habit of making it. The farmhouse goat’s cheese of these hills had almost gone when a recovery project in 1981, credited by the Generalitat to the back-to-the-land generation, fixed its form: goat’s milk, three weeks’ maturing at least, one-kilo wheels sized to the old clay moulds. Round Sant Miquel de Campmajor the young makers learned to coax the grey-blue bloom that gives the rind its ash colour and the paste its hazelnut finish.", "where": "Cal Formatger on Carrer de la Cort Reial: ask what came in from the Garrotxa that week."}, {"id": "ratafia", "pattern": "ratafia", "tag": "Food gem · Drink", "name": "Ratafia", "story": "Every Catalan house that makes ratafia guards its own recipe; the oldest on paper, from 1842, comes from La Selva — the comarca whose capital, Santa Coloma de Farners, has run a ratafia competition since 1982. The walnuts are picked green around Sant Joan in late June, before the shell hardens, and steeped in spirit with herbs and spice for months. It is the IGP Ratafia Catalana now, and still a farmhouse solstice ritual: bottled at midsummer, ready by winter.", "where": "Santa Coloma de Farners, half an hour south-west; elsewhere, look for the IGP seal on the bottle."}, {"id": "esmorzar-de-forquilla", "pattern": "esmorzar de forquilla", "tag": "Food gem · Tradition", "name": "Esmorzar de forquilla", "story": "The fork breakfast is working Catalonia’s second breakfast: a bite before dawn, then, mid-morning, tripe, pig’s trotters, stewed botifarra and a glass of red. Food writers date its consolidation to the mid-eighteenth century, with the markets at its centre — which is why Girona still does it properly. In 2024 the tradition got its own book, Laia Freixinet’s Esmorzars de forquilla. Round Girona it never needed reviving; in the villages it simply never stopped.", "where": "The market house opposite the Mercat del Lleó from nine, or the roadside bar on the Banyoles road from six — both in the tables."}];
